From f53c956337a01d0a9a96de9f8b9212c68f993aa6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 04:40:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] feat: navigation follows the product, not the row count
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A section appears in the navigation when the feature exists, and that is a
constant in code changed by a phase commit — never a query. The difference
is the client: a menu that shows up when the first row is created and
disappears when the last one is deleted makes the system's architecture
blink at someone who is only trying to follow their own project. When
phase 10 turns content on, it appears for everyone, including clients with
zero pieces, who get an honest empty state and still know where the section
lives.

Two sections exist today, so two are listed. The other five keep their
slugs and their routes: hiding a section from the navigation never
invalidates a deep link, and email is the way most clients arrive.

Onboarding is not a section and will not become one. It is a task, reached
from the attention block and from the journey stage. A pending item that
becomes a menu entry stops being pending and becomes a place.

The bottom bar only pays for itself with three destinations. With two it
would spend fifty-six permanent pixels plus the gesture area to offer one
link the home already gives, and its "More" panel would have nothing to
open. Below the threshold the header's word row serves both breakpoints —
and the height reservation in main disappears with the bar, or a phantom
footer stays behind.

Cycle left the header. It means something inside the journey and nothing
inside an application bar, so it moved down to the block that gives it
context.

